            0.297
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3.6 Inverse Proportions"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.306,
            0.845,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you recall the rule of three? When two ratios are proportional, i.e., when"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.346,
            0.845,
            0.411
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a : b : : c : d, then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d = { \\frac { b c } { a } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . We call such proportions direct proportions. We use this understanding to find the value of the fourth quantity (d), when the value of three quantities (a, b, and c) are given."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.42,
            0.845,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 1:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If 5 workers can move 4500 bricks in a day, how many workers are needed to move 18000 bricks in a day?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.463,
            0.846,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This can be represented as a statement of proportionality — 4500 : 18000 :: 5 : x. We can find the value of x by"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.413,
            0.501,
            0.585,
            0.53
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "x = \\frac{18000 \\times 5}{4500} = 20"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.532,
            0.584,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, the number of workers needed are 20."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.562,
            0.846,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 2:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Puneeth’s father went from Lucknow to Kanpur in 3 hours by riding his motorcycle at a speed of 30 km/h. If he takes a car instead and drives at 60 km/h, how long will it take him to reach Kanpur?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.627,
            0.801,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Can we represent this problem with the following statement of proportionality — 30 : 60 :: 3 : x ? Will the travel time increase or decrease as the speed of the motorcycle increases?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.815,
            0.622,
            0.902,
            0.687
          ],
